ore generally if start >= end? Intervals likely start <= end. For first interval, current[0] < last[1] true if start < end, so no duplicate. But if start == end, duplicate. But bug is redundant/incorrect because it processes first interval again; should iterate intervals[1:]. Also if sort by end, first may not be smallest start. But even with sort by start, processing first again can duplicate for zero-length intervals. Also if intervals are tuples immutable? last[1] assignment fails if tuples. But function uses list intervals? It says intervals likely lists. Could be bug: mutating input? Not necessarily. Need at least two bugs. The obvious: sort by end not start; loop over all intervals instead of intervals[1:] (or merged initialized empty). Also condition for touching intervals: user says touching intervals like [1,3] and [3,5].", "top2": [[[1596, -0.001175796496681869], [760, -8.114876747131348]], [[1144, -1.5974172129062936e-05], [599, -11.662922859191895]], [[4087, -0.009997887536883354], [5707, -4.6750922203063965]], [[1156, -0.0035652818623930216], [303, -5.7612080574035645]], [[579, -0.8992486000061035], [13, -1.3808578252792358]], [[1622, -0.031571146100759506], [3296, -4.0415239334106445]], [[13, -0.03457309678196907], [25, -3.5421032905578613]], [[14235, -0.11420012265443802], [26472, -3.263366222381592]], [[1423, -1.0838356018066406], [22903, -1.4585437774658203]], [[21851, -0.5162911415100098], [1396, -0.9741082191467285]], [[303, -0.18408967554569244], [11, -1.8418904542922974]], [[12654, -0.2716027498245239], [2574, -2.0661661624908447]], [[709, -0.26822802424430847], [10562, -1.4583563804626465]], [[11, -0.9412396550178528], [10562, -1.111161470413208]], [[10033, -0.0024481555446982384], [1754, -6.442736625671387]], [[1754, -0.0026410496793687344], [799, -5.9955058097839355]], [[799, -0.6297593712806702], [303, -0.7756791710853577]], [[303, -0.048724010586738586], [799, -3.2221171855926514]], [[799, -4.0531240301788785e-06], [11316, -13.4801864624023